# Weekly maintenance cadence — DAILY 07:00 UTC, effectively weekly (MAINTENANCE
# HAS STRICT PRIORITY over bootstrap).
#
# The maintenance cycle is the WEEKLY-PRIORITY provider worker: it fetches
# TIME_SERIES_WEEKLY for a newly-closed week and rolls each symbol's
# technical_metrics anchor forward. It NEVER depends on split reconciliation
# (separate low-frequency SPLITS responsibility) and never blocks on it.
#
#   MONDAY 07:00 UTC -> WEEKLY refresh for the just-closed (Friday) week.
#   TUE-SAT 07:00 UTC -> automatic catch-up ONLY if Monday's pass was missed
#                       (is_weekly_phase_ready is Mon-Sat); when the cycle is
#                       complete the run performs ZERO provider calls (noop).
#
# Rationale for a DAILY timer with weekly-effective work (vs a single Monday
# trigger): the 200W SMA basis only changes when a new week closes, so the
# provider requests are inherently weekly — a single Monday pass fetches ~50
# requests (one per symbol) within the two-key 50/day budget. But a daily timer
# that no-ops when complete gives AUTOMATIC CATCH-UP: if Monday's pass fails
# (outage, quota), Tuesday simply resumes it instead of leaving the anchor stuck
# for a second week. This is strictly more robust and costs ZERO extra provider
# requests (idempotent completed cycles make no calls — see tests). Bootstrap,
# which runs AFTER this (08:00 UTC), can therefore never starve it.
[Unit]
Description=Stock Autotrader history maintenance (daily, effectively weekly WEEKLY refresh; priority over bootstrap)

[Timer]
OnCalendar=*-*-* 07:00:00 UTC
RandomizedDelaySec=15m
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target